ALEPH-NULL  (ℵ₀)  —  STRUCTURE
============================================================
511.3 Set theory (cardinals, ordinals, ℵ₀, continuum c, etc.)

ℵ₀ (pronounced “aleph-null” or “aleph-zero”) is the 
smallest infinite cardinal number in set theory. Formally, 
it is defined as the cardinality of the set of natural 
numbers N = {0,1,2,3,… }. That is: ∣N∣ = ℵ₀
​
This concept was introduced by Georg Cantor in the late 19th 
century and serves as the foundation for the hierarchy of 
infinite cardinalities.

Definition:
  ℵ₀ = the cardinality class of all sets admitting a bijection
        with the natural numbers N = {0,1,2,3,...}.

Structural Interpretation:
  ℵ₀ = |ω|
    where:
      ω = the first infinite ordinal
      |·| = the cardinal-collapse map (forgetting order)

  Thus:
      ω + ω = ω·2 ≠ ω   ordinal ω  → encodes order-type
      |ω + ω| = ℵ₀      cardinal ℵ₀ → encodes size-type
  Also,
      |ω × ω| = ℵ₀
  via Cantor's diagonal (pairing) bijection.

Set-Theoretic Models:
ℵ₀ is tied to the notion of well-orderings. Any set that can 
be put in a bijection with N is said to be countably infinite, 
and its elements can be indexed by ordinals less than ω, where
ω is the first infinite ordinal.

Countable Sets:
  A set X is countable  ⇔  ∃ bijection f : X → N.
  Examples:
    - N (naturals)
    - Z (integers)
    - Q (rationals)
  All have cardinality ℵ₀.

Uncountable Sets:
  Some sets cannot be indexed by N.
  Example:
    - R (reals) has cardinality c > ℵ₀.

Algebra of ℵ₀:
  Finite additions:
      ℵ₀ + n = ℵ₀
      ℵ₀ · n = ℵ₀

  Countable additions/products:
      ℵ₀ + ℵ₀ = ℵ₀
      ℵ₀ · ℵ₀ = ℵ₀

  Structural reason:
      ω ⊔ ω   has order-type ω
      ω × ω   has a bijection with ω (diagonal enumeration)

Why ℵ₀ is “smallest infinite”:
  - No finite cardinal equals ℵ₀.
  - Any infinite cardinal κ satisfies κ ≥ ℵ₀.
  - ℵ₀ is the initial infinite fixed point of bijection-size.
    : ℵ₀ is the first invariant produced by the cardinal-
      collapse operator.

Examples beyond numbers
Prime numbers: Countably infinite, ∣Primes∣ = ℵ₀
Finite-length strings over a finite alphabet (e.g., all words 
in English) are countable: ℵ₀
Rational numbers Q are countably infinite despite being dense 
in R.

Summary:
  ℵ₀ is the base unit of infinite cardinality:
      the size of any set indexable by ω,
      stable under finite and countable algebraic operations,
      and strictly smaller than the continuum c.

