CANTOR EXCISION NOTE — ℵ₀ CONTEXT
============================================================

Purpose
-------
This note clarifies the role of Cantorian cardinality theory
within the ALEPH-NULL (ℵ₀) structure sheet, and explains why
Cantor’s hierarchy is *mathematically valid* but *ontologically
incompatible* with a relational operator stack.

Scope
-----
The ℵ₀ sheet is a classical set-theoretic reference document.
It describes:

    • ordinals (ω, ω₁, …)
    • cardinals (ℵ₀, ℵ₁, …)
    • collapse operators (|·|)
    • continuum cardinal c
    • diagonalization and bijection theory

These belong to standard ZFC mathematics and are included here
for structural completeness only.

Excision Principle
------------------
Cantorian objects (ℵ₀, 2^ℵ₀, c, etc.) are *not* permitted as
ontological commitments.

Reason:
    • RDG requires finite foundations.
    • Q□ is not a set.
    • G is a coarse-grained quotient, not a power-set object.
    • I is finite-resolution and partitionable.
    • Q is rational and many-to-one.
    • Operate only on finite relational configurations.
    • Infinite behavior must arise from finite-resolution limits,
      never from completed infinite sets.

Thus:
    Cantor’s hierarchy is valid *mathematically*,
    but invalid *as a model of relational ontology*.

Allowed Use
-----------
Cantor’s theory may appear:

    • in classical reference sheets (such as this ℵ₀ document)
    • in historical or structural explanations
    • in comparisons between ordinal and cardinal modes
    • in operator-form descriptions of collapse maps

Forbidden Use
-------------
Cantor’s hierarchy may *not* appear:

    • in definitions of Q□, G, R, I, Q, RDG, or MFE
    • in any operator pipeline
    • as a literal size of Image(R)
    • as a foundational object in generator or meso layers
    • as a cardinality claim about observables

Constructive Replacement
------------------------
Whenever continuum richness is required, it must be
expressed constructively:

    For every finite resolution n ∈ ℕ,
    there exist ≥ 2^n distinguishable meso-layer states.

This preserves continuum-like behavior without invoking
completed infinities.

Summary
-------
Cantor’s ℵ₀ and the continuum c belong to classical set theory.
They do *not* belong to the relational ontology.

